\version "2.24.0"
% c414 (Adler PDF 270, Copland: a diminuendo orchestrated by cutting doublers; PDF 245, Strauss: the sustained parts stop for a moment so the answering line is heard; PDF 249-250: a2 unisons are for tutti volume): Fl 2, Ob 2 and Tpt 2 rest at 217-218 after the diminuendo, so the cor anglais and Hn 1-2 echo of theme B (A flat3-G flat4) is not covered by two trumpets and doubled unisons at f; they re-enter on the sub. p chord at 219.
% c258 (expression; Sebesky ch. 1 Ex. 2A, PDF 17-18, and ch. 6 PDF 204: a line handed to another colour gets its own word): 211 theme B (movement II's cor anglais song) in Tpt 1-2, Fl 1-2, Ob 1, Cl 1 is marked "cantabile", as its echo at 217 is "espress."; Ob 2 and Cl 2 take the accent of the downbeat with the rest of their staff (PDF 214-215, Ex. 281). Dynamics unchanged.
% CODA, bars 211-222 (c013: 211-214 augmented to 211-218; old 215-218 are now 219-222, every later bar +4). 211-218: the D flat episode, ff, in the trumpets: theme B's single recall from movement II, its second half,
% ending on D flat (mvt2 c100 sketch; cyclic-notes: give the forte D flat to the trumpets, not movement II's bar 61 tutti). D flat over F
% at 213-214 is the Neapolitan sixth of C. 215: German sixth of C spelled with D sharp in the violas; 216: cadential 6/4, the violas' D sharp
% rises to E natural (cyclic-notes 'Owed' item 9: the E flat/E natural thread closed in the violas with an augmented sixth); 217: V7;
% c013: theme B augmented (note values doubled, 8 bars, about 13 s at 2 = 72 against movement II's 16 s; was 6.7 s), so it is heard as the theme
% and not as a fast figure. Harmony per old half bar now per bar: D flat (211-212, re-struck f at 212), G flat maj7 (213: the F of trumpet 2 is the
% chord's 7th and falls to E flat in 214; no G flat above it), A flat 7 (214), F minor 7 (215), D flat over F (216-218, the scale in 215-216).
% 218 (c002): movement I's stop chord B-D-F-A flat itself, D in the bass (vii°6/5 over the timpani's G), so the arrival is not a root-position V-I (cadence plan: the first is 262-263 since v14; c070: these are v01 numbers, 218 = now 222, 219 = now 223). Top voice D flat - C - B (- C at 219).

% c028: 211-218 theme B phrased in two halves with a breath after 214, as movement II phrases it (mvt II bSec, breath between the halves);
% the upper woodwinds that double the trumpets (Fl 1-2, Ob 1, Cl 1, and Ob 2/Cl 2 on their shared staves) stay ff with them instead of dropping to f (Adler II PDF 188-189: doubling at pitch strengthens the tune)
% c042 (harmony; A&S PDF 380, 393, 388): 222 the Fl 1/Vln I run into C7 is G-A flat-B (was G-A natural-B against the held A flat of the stop chord: an augmented octave, and flat 6 replaced by natural 6 before it reaches 5).
% The augmented second A flat-B is an arpeggiation inside the diminished seventh (A&S PDF 380).
% c105 (MOLA PDF 5: parts are read by one player; Gould PDF 66-67 is the score-only reason): the tied minims of c035 (211 Ob 2, Cl 2; 215 Hn 3, Hn 4) show a
% crossing or unison on the shared staves only; in the single-player parts they were printed as minim~minim where a semibreve fills the bar. Parts now read a semibreve (tag 'part).
% c108 (expression; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 129: an echo is weaker than the first statement but akin to it; PDF 133: between brass and brass the harmonic basis must be of inferior dynamic power): 217-218 Hn 1-2 a2 on the echo (Hn 2 left its held D flat 4, which Hn 1 met at the unison), and the held F/A flat chord in Tpt 3, Hn 3-4, Tbn 1-2, B.tbn, tuba, bassoons and contrabassoon diminishes f > mp, so the echo is heard; these parts enter 219 at p (not sub. p).
% c108 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 130: a prolonged crescendo adds strings, then wood-wind, then brass; the brass make the final crescendo into the climax): 220-222 the brass hold p through 220, crescendo from 221 and reach mf at 222 (strings, wood-wind and timpani keep p < mp < f < fff); sub. p 219 and fff 223 unchanged.
% c178 (expression; Adler ch. 7, adler1 PDF 188: the flute needs a great deal of breath, give it rest after sustained passages, as Brahms 1/iv gives the held note to one flute while the other breathes; PDF 186: above A6 the flute must be approached with care): 222 Fl 2 releases its F5 on beat 4 (was a whole note into 223), so it breathes before its fff G6 at 223, as Fl 1 does on its eighth rest before the scale (Fl 2 held 219-222 without a breath, about 7 s in the allargando, then the fff bar).
% c338 (expression; Casanelles ch. 4, PDF 149-150: a line held at one level sounds lifeless; PDF 155: the lyrical line swells into its note of arrival): 212-214 theme B swells to its peak (A flat at 213) and recedes, as movement II
%   shapes it (C.a. 41-42, p < mp at 42.1, > from 42.4), in every part that plays the tune (Tpt 1-2, Fl 1-2, Ob 1, Cl 1, Vln I) and in Ob 2, Cl 2 (shared staves) and Vln II (the tune's thirds). Levels unchanged: ff 211, f in the strings, > f 216-217, sub. p 219.
% c368 (MOLA PDF 10, Example 3: a hairpin ends on a printed level; PDF 11, Example 6, and PDF 8: what is played is printed, and one attack carries one mark in every staff): c338's hidden levels are printed: ff at 215 in Fl 1-2, Ob 1-2, Cl 1-2, Tpt 1-2 (after the 214 diminuendo, so the player knows the swell returns to ff before the 216 diminuendo to f), ff at 213 and f at 215 in Vln I-II (ends of their f < and > hairpins). MIDI unchanged.
codaTwoTptOne = { aes'2->(\ff^\markup \italic "cantabile" des''2 | f''2.\< ges''4 | aes''2\! bes''4 aes''4 | ges''2.\> f''4) \breathe | es''2.(\ff f''4 | des''1~\> | des''1~\f | des''1) \breathe | c''1\subP | c''1 | b'1\< | b'1\mf\< | }
codaTwoTptTwo = { f'2->(\ff-\tag #'part ^\markup \italic "cantabile" aes'2 | des''2.\< es''4 | f''2\! ges''4 f''4 | es''2.\> des''4) \breathe | c''2.(\ff des''4 | aes'1)\> | R1\! | R1 | fis'1\subP | g'1 | f'1\< | aes'1\mf\< | }
% c090 (cyclic lens; Adler ch. 17, PDF 264 and 267 point 4: no instrument of a competing colour on the solo's pitch): 220 Tpt 3 holds C4 (was a leap C4-E4 onto the thread pitch E4
%   at sub. p, at the unison with the violas' and Hn 3's D sharp4-E4); E4 at 220 now comes only from D sharp.
% c104 (REJECTED by the composer: Tpt 3 stays B3 at 221 so the 6/4 fourth resolves down, cycle 102) (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 100: close order without gaps; PDF 114: balance matters most in sustained chords): 221 Tpt 3 D4 (was B3, a third B3 with Hn 2 and Hn 4), so V7 has its fifth in the brass; D4 is held into the stop chord at 222.
codaTwoTptThree = { des'1\ff\> | des'1\f | ges'1 | es'1 \breathe | aes'1 | f'1 | f'1~\> | f'1\mp \breathe | c'1~\p | c'1 | b1\< | d'1\mf\< | }
% c046 (Adler PDF 292, 303: periodic rests, after high playing): Hn 1-2 rest 211-214 (they played every bar 199-228, about 50 s, with written B flat 5 / G5 held at 207-210)
% c107 (texture; Adler ch. 5, PDF 135: the background moves under the melody's long notes; PDF 142-143: middleground in its own rhythm): 217-218 Hn 1 answers the trumpets' theme-B head (A flat-D flat-F-G flat, as at 211-212, one octave lower: movement II's own pitch, cor anglais 41) under the held D flat, and reaches its A flat4 of 219; the held D flat/F chord no longer stands still for two bars.
codaTwoHnOne = { R1*4 | aes'1\f | aes'1 | aes2(^\markup \italic "espress." des'2 | f'2. ges'4) \breathe | aes'1\subP | g'1 | g'1\< | f'1\mf\< | }
% c110 (cyclic lens; A&S Unit 32, PDF 589 and 591, Ex. 32-10a: a V7 reinterpreted as a German sixth moves the music down a half step, the held tone respelled; PDF 588: a true enharmonic is heard
%   whatever the notation; PDF 597-598 and 600-602: an enharmonic pitch link carried in one voice integrates a remote area and enlarges a motive): 219-221 Hn 2 holds the echo's G flat 4
%   as F sharp 4 (the German sixth's sharp 4) and rises to G4 (was a leap G flat 4 -> C4). Hn 2 held the same G flat 4 as the 7th of A flat 7 (V7 of D flat) at 209-210; the D flat episode is
%   now framed by that chord and its German-sixth respelling in the same player. Hn 1 still continues the theme to A flat 4; both horns close on G4 at 220. 221 Hn 2 D4, the V7's fifth (no D in the brass before).
% c110 (A&S PDF 585: rhythm, texture and dynamics shape how a change of key is heard; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 129: an echo is softer than the statement): 216-217 the tune's held D flat (Tpt 1-2, Fl 1-2, Ob 1-2, Cl 1-2)
%   goes ff > f, so the horns' f echo of theme B at 217-218 is not under a ff unison; ff at 211 and sub. p at 219 unchanged.
% c138: 'espress.' also on Hn 2 at 217, because the part-combined a2 staff printed neither horn's 'espress.' (only Hn 1 carried it).
codaTwoHnTwo = { R1*4 | c'1\f | des'1 | aes2(^\markup \italic "espress." des'2 | f'2. ges'4) \breathe | fis'1\subP | g'1 | d'1\< | d'1\mf\< | }
% c035 (Gould PDF 65, 67): 215-218 Hn 3 (F3) lies below Hn 4 (A flat 3); 215 is written as tied minims so the stems show the crossing that a semibreve chord hid. Sound unchanged.
% c136 (playability; Adler ch. 9, PDF 292: frequent rests so the brass lips recover): Hn 3-4 rest 215-218 (Hn 3 was in unison with Tbn 1, Hn 4 with Bn 1); Hn 4 otherwise played 199-264 with one bar's rest (229), about 110 s. They re-enter p at 219, the German sixth.
% c305 (Gould, Behind Bars ch. 2, PDF 66: keep noteheads separate; a semibreve must not appear joined to a stem; editions reverse the order so the semibreve is never beside a stem): score-only force-hshift at 214 (Hn 3-4, a second) puts the semibreve on the stem-free side of the other part's note, 0.15-0.2 space apart
codaTwoHnThree = { aes1\ff\> | aes'4(\f ges'4 f'4 es'4) | des'1 | \partCombineApart \tag #'score { \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #1.1 } aes4( bes4 c'2) \breathe | \partCombineAutomatic R1*4 | dis'1\p | e'1 | f'1\< | d'1\mf\< | }
% c104 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 102-103: horns in pairs against single trombones and trumpets): 211-212 Hn 4 A flat 3 with Hn 3 (was F3, unison with Tbn 1); the fifth no longer rests on one horn.
codaTwoHnFour = { aes1\ff\> | aes'4(\f ges'4 f'4 es'4) | bes1 | \tag #'score { \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #0 } ges1 \breathe | R1*4 | c'1\p | c'1 | b1\< | b1\mf\< | }
codaTwoTbnOne = { f1\ff\> | f1\f | ges1 | es1 \breathe | f1 | f1 | f1~\> | f1\mp \breathe | fis1\p | g1 | g1\< | aes1\mf\< | }
% c264 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 85: chords follow the harmonic scale, wide intervals low, closer ones higher; Casanelles PDF 259, 274-275: the low partials of a bass are octave and fifth, a third only from the 5th partial up):
%   215-218 Tbn 2 no longer holds A flat2 a minor third above the bass trombone's F2 (with tuba F1 and Tbn 1 F3). It keeps C3 from 214 at 215 (F minor 7: F2-C3-F3, partials 2-3-4 of F1),
%   rises to D flat3 for the D flat 6/3 chord at 216-218 and falls to C3 at 219, as every doubled D flat falls there. A flat stays in Bn 1, the horns, trumpets and violas. Dynamics unchanged.
codaTwoTbnTwo = { des1\ff\> | des1\f | des1 | c1 \breathe | c1 | des1 | des1~\> | des1\mp \breathe | c1\p | c1 | b,1\< | b,1\mf\< | }
% c316 (playability; Adler adler1 PDF 292: brass need frequent rests, and 'in a loud passage one should not phrase too many notes on one breath in a slow tempo'; adler2 PDF 8: the tuba takes 'a great deal of breath',
%   'special care must be exercised not to tire out the performer'): 220|221 bass trombone and tuba breathe between the two G's, before the crescendo; 219-225 (p < fff, 2 = 72 then 56, about 13 s) had no breath.
codaTwoBassTbn = { des1\ff\> | des1\f | ges,1 | aes,1 \breathe | f,1 | f,1 | f,1~\> | f,1\mp \breathe | aes,1\p | g,1 \breathe | g,1\< | d1\mf\< | }
% c406 (playability; Adler ch. 10, adler2 PDF 8: the tuba 'takes a great deal of breath ... Frequent rests are strongly recommended between passages'; adler1 PDF 292: frequent rests so the brass lips recover): 217-218 the tuba rests (was F1 held f > mp with a breath after 218). It played 211-228 without a rest (about 33 s, ff to fff, breaths only) after 199-210; F1 stays in the contrabassoon, F2 in the bass trombone, double basses and bassoon 2 under the horns' echo; it fades at 216 and re-enters p at 219 with the low brass.
codaTwoTuba = { des,1\ff\> | des,1\f | ges,,1 | aes,,1 \breathe | f,,1 | f,,1\> | R1\! | R1 | aes,,1\p | g,,1 \breathe | g,,1\< | d,1\mf\< | }
% c045 (Adler part 2 PDF 80, Ex. XII-48: a longer roll is written as tied notes; an untied roll restarts at each barline): rolls on one pitch tied across the barlines
% c236 (Adler ch. 12, PDF 79, 81): 211-212 the timpani strike the D-flat tonic with the bass (was A-flat, the fifth, after the A-flat roll 209-210); 214 A-flat unchanged (bass A-flat).
% 215 the D-flat drum returns to C in the four Largamente bars of rest, so the drum that sounded the Neapolitan D-flat sounds C at 223 (D-flat -> C, with Tpt 1 B -> C and timpani G -> C at 222-223 unchanged).
codaTwoTimp = { des4->\ff r4 r2 | des4\f r4 r2 | R1 | aes,4 r4 aes,4 r4 | R1^\markup \small \italic \column { "muta D♭ in C" "medium sticks" } | R1*3 | aes,1:32\subP | g,1:32\<~ | g,1:32\mp\<~ | g,1:32\f\< | }
% c036 (Adler PDF 186: above A6 the flute's notes are approached best by scale): 222 Fl 1 joins Vln I's run G6-A6-B6 into the C7 at 223 (was a leap B5-C7 at fff).
% c195 (Gould, Behind Bars PDF 35: when all the notes of a beam lie beyond the first ledger line, an interval wider than a second takes a 1/2-space slope; PDF 36: the beam follows the outside interval):
% 222 Fl 1 G6-A flat 6-B6 had a horizontal beam (score: stems up on the shared staff; part: stems down), while Vln I has the 1/2-space rise on the same notes since c105. \offset adds the slope to the default position in both. Sound unchanged.
codaTwoFluteOne = { aes''2->(\ff^\markup \italic "cantabile" des'''2 | f'''2.\< ges'''4 | aes'''2\! bes'''4 aes'''4 | ges'''2.\> f'''4) \breathe | es'''2.(\ff f'''4 | des'''1~\> | des'''1~\f | des'''1) \breathe | c'''1\subP | c'''1\< | b''1\mp\< | b''2\f\< r8 \once \offset positions #'(-0.25 . 0.25) Beam g'''8( aes''' b''') | }
% c385 (Gould PDF 50-51): 222.4 Fl 2's crotchet rest sat on the bottom of the stave, two spaces below the centre and four below its own F5; Fl 1 is on ledger lines
%   above, so the rest moves back to the centre (score only).
codaTwoFluteTwo = { aes''2->(\ff-\tag #'part ^\markup \italic "cantabile" des'''2 | f'''2.\< ges'''4 | aes'''2\! bes'''4 aes'''4 | ges'''2.\> f'''4) \breathe | es'''2.(\ff f'''4 | des'''1)\> | R1\! | R1 | fis''1\subP | g''1\< | f''1\mp\< | f''2.\f\< \tag #'score { \once \override Rest.extra-offset = #'(0 . 2) } r4\! | }
% c021: 211-218 Fl 1-2 a2 double trumpet 1's theme B an octave above (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 75: in the top register two flutes suffice to double a trumpet at the upper octave)
% c019: 211-218 Ob 1 and Cl 1 double trumpet 1's theme B at the unison (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 74-75: a wood-wind added to a brass melody gives a finer legato; Ex. 78, 93-94)
% c080 (cyclic; A&S Unit 28, PDF 491-492 and 499 point 3: flat 2 tends to move down; PDF 496-497: a chromatic step is written as a minor 2nd, not on the same staff degree): 219 Ob 1 C5 (was D sharp 5).
%   Ob 1 doubles Tpt 1's theme B at the unison through 218; its D flat 5 now falls to C with Tpt 1 (D flat - C - B - C, protected top line) instead of rising to D sharp 5 on the same staff degree. The thread's D sharp stays in the violas and Hn 3 (D sharp 4).
codaTwoOboeOne = { aes'2->(\ff^\markup \italic "cantabile" des''2 | f''2.\< ges''4 | aes''2\! bes''4 aes''4 | ges''2.\> f''4) \breathe | es''2.(\ff f''4 | des''1~\> | des''1~\f | des''1) \breathe | c''1\subP | e''1\< | d''1\mp\< | d''1\f\< | }
% c010: 215 Ob 1 and Hn 3 spelled D sharp like the violas: E flat (Tpt 1, 213) becomes D sharp and rises to E (A&S PDF 588, 597-598).
% c035 (Gould PDF 65-67): 211 Ob 2 and Cl 2 as tied minims, so Ob 2's F5 above Ob 1's A flat is not read as one chord and Cl 2's unison A flat does not touch Cl 1's stem. Sound unchanged.
% c052 (harmony; A&S Unit 24, PDF 406-408): 214 Ob 2 resolves the 7th G flat to F with Ob 1 on beat 4 instead of holding it against the resolution.
% c152 (A&S Unit 21, PDF 352 sec. 12: avoid sounding the tone of resolution against the suspension): 211 Ob 2 D flat 5 on beats 1-2 (was F5), so the nearest F under Vln I's suspended G flat 6 (a minor ninth below) waits for its resolution on beat 2; Ob 2 takes F5 on beat 3. The F4/F3 of Tpt 2, violas, Bn 1 and Tbn 1 lie an octave and more lower (PDF 352: tolerated in a contrasting texture).
codaTwoOboeTwo = { des''2->\ff f''2 | f''1\< | des''1\! | ges''2.\> f''4 \breathe | es''1\ff | des''1\> | R1\! | R1 | c''1\subP | e''1\< | d''1\mp\< | d''1\f\< | }
% c130 (Casanelles ch. 5, PDF 226-227 and 236: the instrument is part of a theme's meaning; Debussy's cor anglais with trumpet as a signifying blend): 217-220 the cor anglais, theme B's own instrument in movement II (41-44), joins Hn 1 on the answer at movement II's own pitch (A flat 3-D flat 4-F4-G flat 4), then A flat 4 (219) and G4 (220) with Hn 1. Its only notes after 186.
% c345 (MOLA PDF 5: logical cues during long periods of rest, audible and obvious, in the reading key; Example 4, PDF 10): part-only cue, tag 'part; score and MIDI unchanged.
codaTwoCorAnglais = { R1*4 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "tptOne" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Tpt. 1" } } | aes2(\f^\markup \italic "espress." des'2 | f'2. ges'4) | aes'1\subP | g'2 r2 | R1*2 | }
codaTwoClOne = { aes'2->(\ff^\markup \italic "cantabile" des''2 | f''2.\< ges''4 | aes''2\! bes''4 aes''4 | ges''2.\> f''4) \breathe | es''2.(\ff f''4 | des''1~\> | des''1~\f | des''1) \breathe | aes'1\subP | g'1\< | g'1\mp\< | aes'1\f\< | }
% c060 (cyclic lens; A&S Unit 29, PDF 504: the augmented sixth's sharp 4 is a passing tone to 5; Sebesky ch. 1, PDF 13-14, focus): 220 Cl 2 and Vln I (lower) take F sharp up to G, so the only E4 arriving by step from outside is the violas' D sharp -> E (with Hn 3), the thread's own voice
codaTwoClTwo = { \tag #'score { aes'2->\ff~ aes'2 } \tag #'part { aes'1->\ff } | aes'1\< | ges'1\! | aes'1\> \breathe | f'1\ff | f'1\> | f'1~\f | f'1 \breathe | fis'1\subP | g'1\< | f'1\mp\< | f'1\f\< | }
% c289 (A&S Unit 20, PDF 329 Ex. 20-2b: a chordal skip breaks up parallel 5ths): 213 Bn 1 B flat3 -> G flat3 on beat 3 (a skip within the G flat chord, unison with Tbn 1), so it no longer moves B flat3-A flat3 in 12ths with the tune's passing F5-E flat5 (Tpt 2, Vln II) into 214.
codaTwoBsnOne = { f1\ff\> | aes4(\f ges4 f4 es4 | bes2 ges2) | aes4( bes4 c'2) \breathe | aes1 | aes1 | aes1~\> | aes1\mp \breathe | c'1\p | c'1\< | b1\mp\< \breathe | b1\f\< | }
codaTwoBsnTwo = { des1\ff\> | des1\f | ges,1 | aes,1 \breathe | f,1 | f,1 | f,1~\> | f,1\mp \breathe | aes,1\p | g,1\< \breathe | g,1\mp\< | d,1\f\< | }
codaTwoCbsn = { des,1\ff\> | des,1\f | ges,,1 | aes,,1 \breathe | f,,1 | f,,1 | f,,1~\> | f,,1\mp \breathe | aes,,1\p | g,,1\< | g,,1\mp\< \breathe | d,,1\f\< | }
% c051 (melody; Sebesky ch. 6, PDF 211-212, Ex. 279-280: a background line that shadows the tune too closely distorts the focus): 212 and 214 the violins no longer move a beat ahead of theme B
% (214 Vln I F5 against the tune's G flat 5, Vln II D flat 5 against E flat 5; 212 beat 4 A flat 5 against G flat 5); they now take the tune's own note on beat 4 (212 Vln I G flat; 214 Vln I and II dotted minim then F/D flat). Vln II keeps D flat at 212 (E flat there made fifths with Cl 2 into 213).
% c054 (orchestration; Adler ch. 8, PDF 249-250, 265-266): 212-214 Vln I doubles theme B an octave above Tpt 1 with the flutes (8'+4') instead of a shadow line that
% touched the tune at the unison on some beats and ran a third beside it on others (revises c051, keeps its aim: nothing moves against the tune).
% c118 (expression; Adler ch. 2, PDF 37-38: unslurred notes change bow on every note, the only string slurs are bow slurs): 212-214 Vln I-II slurred one bar per bow on theme B, as the
% winds' phrase and the violins' own 215-216 (were detached half notes and quarters); 217-218 Vln I-II and Vc change bow at will on the held f (PDF 39: do not overload the bow in slow passages; the Cb had it since c106).
% c151 (A&S PDF 357-358, Ex. 21-23a): 211 Vln I G flat 6 (tied from 210) resolves to F6 on beat 2 over the D flat chord, then D flat 6-C6 as before (was D flat 6 half); the A flat 7 of 209-210 now reaches theme B in one line, G flat 6 (209, Fl 1) - G flat 6 (210-211, Vln I) - F6.
% c152 (harmony; A&S Unit 28, PDF 491 sec. 2 and 499 point 3: flat 2 on top gives the Neapolitan its greatest intensity, and the line flat 2-1-7 fills the diminished third; Unit 29, PDF 518 and 536 point 3a: the augmented sixth from the chromatic step 4-sharp 4):
%   217-218 Vln I divides, D flat 6 above F5 (was F6 unis.), so the protected top line D flat-C-B-C is the highest voice (Fl 1, Vln I: D flat 6 216-218 - C6 219-220 - B5 221-222 - C7 223) instead of lying a third under the violins' F6, and the lower desk's F5 rises F-F sharp-G (219-220) in one octave (was F6 - F sharp 5).
% c171 (melody; A&S Unit 3, PDF 50 sec. 3: the highest line attracts the accent; PDF 52 sec. 7: a rhythmic group ends on its long note): 211 Vln I holds D flat 6 on beats 3-4 with Fl 1 (was D flat 6-C6 quarters), so the top line meets theme B's first goal note (A flat-D flat, the rising fourth) and holds it, instead of moving to C6 a minor second against the flutes' D flat 6 in the same octave. c151's G flat 6-F6 suspension on beats 1-2 is kept; 212 F6 is reached by the theme's own leap D flat-F.
codaTwoVlnOne = { ges'''4\ff\>( f'''4) des'''2 | f'''2.(\f\< ges'''4) | aes'''2(\ff bes'''4 aes'''4) | ges'''2.(\> f'''4) | es''4(\f f'' ges'' aes'') | bes''( c''' des''' es''') | <f'' des'''>1~\cbawN^\markup \italic "div." | q1 | <fis'' c'''>1\subP | <g'' c'''>1\< | <f'' b''>1\mp\< | b''2\f\<^\markup \italic "unis." r8 \once \override Beam.positions = #'(-0.25 . 0.25) g'''8( aes''' b''') | }
codaTwoVlnTwo = { aes''2\ff\> aes''4 ges''4 | f''2(\f\< des''2) | des''2(\ff ges''4 f''4) | es''2.(\> des''4) | c''4(\f des'' es'' f'') | ges''( aes'' bes'' c''') | des'''1~\cbawN | des'''1 | <c'' fis''>1\subP^\markup \italic "div." | <c'' g''>1\< | <b' g''>1\mp\< | <d'' aes''>1\f\< | }
% c106 (playability; Adler ch. 3, PDF 76 and 88: in orchestral writing only easy double stops, otherwise divisi; PDF 108: the bass bow is shorter, so a lengthy tenuto is
%   marked 'change bow at will'): 211 violas restate 'div.' after the double bar, key change, letter O and page turn (div. since 205, held thirds/fourths 211-218);
%   217-218 basses 'change bow at will' on the tied F (two bars of Largamente at f, about 3.3 s).
% c138 (expression; Casanelles ch. 6, PDF 290: a soft line is heard only when what sounds with it is made quieter, in the hall that is done in the marking): 217-218 violas, cellos and basses diminish f > mp with the held F/A flat chord in the winds and low brass (c108), so the f echo of theme B in Hn 1-2 and the cor anglais (A flat 3-G flat 4) is not under f strings in its own register; they enter 219 at p. Vln I-II (F6/D flat 6, above the echo) keep f and the sub. p at 219.
% c355 (Adler ch. 2, adler1 PDF 42-43, Ex. II-30: loure, a legato stroke with a slight separation, 'used quite often in accompaniments', dashes with a slur per bow): 211-218 the violas' repeated half-note thirds under theme B are paired in one bow per bar (were a new bow on every half note), so the pulse stays but the chords sing with the cantabile tune, as the violas' own portato at 224-228. Pitches, div., dynamics unchanged.
codaTwoVla = { <des' f'>2--(\ff\>^\markup \italic "div." <des' f'>2--) | <des' f'>2--(\f <des' f'>2--) | <ges' bes'>2--( <ges' bes'>2--) | <aes' c''>2--( <aes' c''>2--) | <aes' c''>2--( <aes' c''>2--) | <aes' des''>2--( <aes' des''>2--) | <aes' des''>2--(\> <aes' des''>2--) | <aes' des''>2--(\mp <aes' des''>2--) | <c' dis'>1\p | <c' e'>1\< | <b f'>1\mp\< | <d' f'>1\f\< | }
codaTwoVc = { des2\ff\> des'2 | des2\f des'2 | ges1 | aes1 | f1 | f,1 | f1~\cbawN\> | f1\mp | aes,1\p | g,1\< | g,1\mp\< | d1\f\< | }
codaTwoCb = { des,2\ff\> des2 | des,2\f des2 | ges,1 | aes,1 | f,1 | f,,1 | f,1~\cbawN\> | f,1\mp | aes,,1\p | g,,1\< | g,,1\mp\< | d,1\f\< | }
% c075 (MOLA PDF 5: logical cues in long rests, audible and obvious, in the reading key; Example 4, PDF 10): part-only cue, tag 'part (score.ly removes it from the score); word cues (the drum staff takes no cue notes) at 221-222 before the first crash at 223, after 222 bars
codaTwoPerc = \drummode { R1*8 | R1*2 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { R1^\markup \tiny "Tpt. 1: B, cresc." | R1^\markup \tiny "Fl. 1: scale" } | }
